[thin] Unavoidable passage rims, and a lint misreading at their edge: the water-in and soda tubes cross the cup's floor and top planes partway round their R14 bends, so each passage meets those planes at an angle. At the top the native B-rep is one plane meeting one torus along a 57° rim; at the floor entries the rim is about 27°. The sub-0.01 mm readings are the rim edges themselves, which the lint reads as material; the 2.26 mm readings are the same wedges a little way in.
alternatives: A vertical crossing needs each bend finished 14 mm below the cup's top, under its own floor, or a taller cap that moves the cap face. A tighter bend breaks the passage's R14 tube radius. A local counterbore at a rim adds a step and a ledge without removing the wedge.
